WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.116 --> 00:00:02.531
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:02.705 --> 00:00:06.560
In today's lesson, we are going to explore four important

3
00:00:06.653 --> 00:00:10.867
separation techniques used in G C S E Chemistry:

4
00:00:11.204 --> 00:00:14.919
filtration, crystallisation, simple distillation

5
00:00:15.197 --> 00:00:17.287
and fractional distillation.

6
00:00:17.624 --> 00:00:21.281
We will examine how each method works, explain when it should be

7
00:00:21.351 --> 00:00:24.997
used, and apply our knowledge to practical examination

8
00:00:25.066 --> 00:00:25.809
questions.

9
00:00:26.518 --> 00:00:28.782
Let us begin with an important principle.

10
00:00:29.606 --> 00:00:33.472
Mixtures contain two or more substances that are not chemically

11
00:00:33.541 --> 00:00:34.528
bonded together.

12
00:00:34.865 --> 00:00:38.742
Because the substances retain their own physical properties, they

13
00:00:38.801 --> 00:00:41.854
can often be separated using physical methods.

14
00:00:42.028 --> 00:00:46.405
The method we choose depends on properties such as solubility,

15
00:00:46.649 --> 00:00:48.936
boiling point and particle size.

16
00:00:49.179 --> 00:00:50.991
Let us begin with filtration.

17
00:00:51.200 --> 00:00:54.996
Filtration is used to separate an insoluble solid from

18
00:00:55.054 --> 00:00:55.693
a liquid.

19
00:00:56.029 --> 00:00:59.013
For example, imagine mixing sand with water.

20
00:00:59.222 --> 00:01:02.972
Sand does not dissolve in water, so the mixture contains

21
00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.967
solid sand particles suspended in the liquid.

22
00:01:06.176 --> 00:01:10.159
To separate them, we pour the mixture through filter paper placed

23
00:01:10.228 --> 00:01:11.482
inside a funnel.

24
00:01:11.633 --> 00:01:15.441
The filter paper contains tiny pores that allow the water to pass

25
00:01:15.488 --> 00:01:18.866
through while retaining the larger sand particles.

26
00:01:19.075 --> 00:01:22.314
The solid left on the filter paper is called the residue.

27
00:01:22.558 --> 00:01:25.739
The liquid collected underneath is called the filtrate.

28
00:01:25.983 --> 00:01:30.279
In this example, sand is the residue and water is the filtrate.

29
00:01:30.673 --> 00:01:33.413
Here is an important examination point.

30
00:01:33.750 --> 00:01:37.558
Filtration cannot be used to remove dissolved salt from water

31
00:01:37.894 --> 00:01:41.551
because the dissolved salt particles pass through the filter

32
00:01:41.610 --> 00:01:42.968
paper with the water.

33
00:01:43.305 --> 00:01:47.113
To separate a dissolved solid from a solution, we need

34
00:01:47.206 --> 00:01:48.506
a different technique.

35
00:01:48.784 --> 00:01:51.095
This brings us to crystallisation.

36
00:01:51.548 --> 00:01:55.960
Crystallisation is used to obtain a dissolved solid from a solution.

37
00:01:56.296 --> 00:01:59.814
For example, imagine that we have salt dissolved in water.

38
00:02:00.209 --> 00:02:03.912
We can gently heat the solution in an evaporating basin

39
00:02:03.994 --> 00:02:05.619
to remove some of the water.

40
00:02:05.898 --> 00:02:09.729
As water evaporates, the solution becomes more concentrated.

41
00:02:10.345 --> 00:02:13.955
When the solution is sufficiently concentrated, we stop heating

42
00:02:14.048 --> 00:02:15.279
and allow it to cool.

43
00:02:15.674 --> 00:02:19.517
As the solution cools, the solubility of many solids

44
00:02:19.563 --> 00:02:22.036
decreases, causing crystals to form.

45
00:02:22.315 --> 00:02:24.857
The crystals can then be separated and dried.

46
00:02:25.136 --> 00:02:28.851
This method is particularly useful when we want to recover a

47
00:02:28.932 --> 00:02:32.021
solid without heating it to complete dryness.

48
00:02:32.264 --> 00:02:35.016
Now, let us consider simple distillation.

49
00:02:35.469 --> 00:02:39.196
Simple distillation is used to separate a solvent from a solution

50
00:02:39.474 --> 00:02:43.259
or to separate liquids with sufficiently different boiling points.

51
00:02:43.654 --> 00:02:46.974
Imagine that we want to obtain pure water from saltwater.

52
00:02:47.125 --> 00:02:50.724
We place the saltwater in a distillation flask and heat it.

53
00:02:50.933 --> 00:02:54.880
As the temperature rises, water evaporates and eventually boils,

54
00:02:55.089 --> 00:02:56.738
producing water vapour.

55
00:02:57.017 --> 00:03:01.138
The dissolved salt does not evaporate significantly under these conditions

56
00:03:01.185 --> 00:03:02.903
and remains in the flask.

57
00:03:03.112 --> 00:03:06.792
The water vapour passes into a condenser, where cold water

58
00:03:06.850 --> 00:03:09.346
flows around the outside of an inner tube.

59
00:03:09.683 --> 00:03:13.514
Energy is transferred from the vapour to the cooling water,

60
00:03:13.665 --> 00:03:17.067
causing the vapour to condense back into liquid water.

61
00:03:17.276 --> 00:03:20.805
The purified water is collected in a separate container.

62
00:03:20.921 --> 00:03:23.801
This collected liquid is called the distillate.

63
00:03:24.079 --> 00:03:28.294
Notice that simple distillation allows us to collect the solvent,

64
00:03:28.410 --> 00:03:32.171
whereas crystallisation is used to obtain the dissolved

65
00:03:32.264 --> 00:03:32.798
solid.

66
00:03:33.042 --> 00:03:35.619
Now, let us examine fractional distillation.

67
00:03:35.736 --> 00:03:39.427
Fractional distillation is used to separate mixtures of miscible

68
00:03:39.486 --> 00:03:41.680
liquids with different boiling points.

69
00:03:41.889 --> 00:03:44.536
Miscible means that the liquids mix completely.

70
00:03:44.710 --> 00:03:47.810
An example is a mixture of ethanol and water.

71
00:03:48.054 --> 00:03:51.966
Ethanol boils at approximately seventy-eight degrees Celsius, while

72
00:03:52.024 --> 00:03:55.716
water boils at approximately one hundred degrees Celsius at

73
00:03:55.798 --> 00:03:57.284
normal atmospheric pressure.

74
00:03:57.528 --> 00:04:01.533
In fractional distillation, the mixture is heated and the vapour

75
00:04:01.603 --> 00:04:03.832
passes through a fractionating column.

76
00:04:03.983 --> 00:04:07.512
The column is hotter at the bottom and cooler at the top.

77
00:04:07.980 --> 00:04:12.090
Repeated evaporation and condensation occur within the column.

78
00:04:12.241 --> 00:04:15.933
The vapour becomes richer in the more volatile substance, which

79
00:04:16.002 --> 00:04:18.928
is the substance that evaporates more readily.

80
00:04:19.381 --> 00:04:23.363
In an ethanol and water mixture, ethanol is collected first

81
00:04:23.538 --> 00:04:25.616
as an ethanol-rich fraction.

82
00:04:26.010 --> 00:04:29.691
Fractional distillation is also used in the petroleum industry

83
00:04:29.969 --> 00:04:33.720
to separate crude oil into fractions with different boiling

84
00:04:33.778 --> 00:04:34.358
ranges.

85
00:04:34.753 --> 00:04:37.446
Now, let us work through a practical example.

86
00:04:37.620 --> 00:04:41.509
Imagine that a student is given a mixture of salt and sand.

87
00:04:41.753 --> 00:04:45.341
The task is to obtain pure, dry salt from the mixture.

88
00:04:45.619 --> 00:04:47.233
How should the student proceed?

89
00:04:47.512 --> 00:04:50.635
First, add water to the mixture and stir thoroughly.

90
00:04:50.878 --> 00:04:55.023
The salt dissolves in the water, but the sand remains insoluble.

91
00:04:55.418 --> 00:04:57.159
Next, filter the mixture.

92
00:04:57.334 --> 00:05:00.596
The sand remains on the filter paper as the residue, while the

93
00:05:00.654 --> 00:05:03.359
salt solution passes through as the filtrate.

94
00:05:03.603 --> 00:05:06.203
Collect the filtrate in an evaporating basin.

95
00:05:06.354 --> 00:05:09.733
Gently heat the solution to evaporate some of the water.

96
00:05:10.069 --> 00:05:13.761
Allow the concentrated solution to cool so that salt crystals

97
00:05:13.819 --> 00:05:14.411
can form.

98
00:05:14.562 --> 00:05:18.370
Finally, separate the crystals from the remaining solution and dry

99
00:05:18.428 --> 00:05:18.695
them.

100
00:05:19.032 --> 00:05:22.643
Notice that we have combined more than one separation technique.

101
00:05:22.886 --> 00:05:26.474
This is very common in G C S E practical questions.

102
00:05:26.869 --> 00:05:28.459
Let us test your understanding.

103
00:05:28.796 --> 00:05:31.861
Which method would you use to separate sand from water?

104
00:05:32.256 --> 00:05:33.010
Filtration.

105
00:05:33.219 --> 00:05:36.888
Which method would you use to obtain pure water from seawater?

106
00:05:37.039 --> 00:05:38.293
Simple distillation.

107
00:05:38.536 --> 00:05:41.764
And which method would you use to separate a mixture of liquids

108
00:05:41.822 --> 00:05:45.688
with different boiling points, such as the fractions in crude oil?

109
00:05:46.025 --> 00:05:47.534
Fractional distillation.

110
00:05:47.871 --> 00:05:51.992
Now, let us tackle a six-mark G C S E-style question.

111
00:05:52.329 --> 00:05:56.044
Describe how a student could obtain pure, dry salt from a mixture

112
00:05:56.079 --> 00:05:57.124
of salt and sand.

113
00:05:57.461 --> 00:06:01.176
A full-mark model answer would be: First, add water to the

114
00:06:01.222 --> 00:06:03.429
mixture and stir to dissolve the salt.

115
00:06:03.765 --> 00:06:05.553
The sand remains insoluble.

116
00:06:05.948 --> 00:06:09.721
Filter the mixture using a funnel and filter paper to remove

117
00:06:09.756 --> 00:06:10.279
the sand.

118
00:06:10.557 --> 00:06:12.763
Collect the salt solution as the filtrate.

119
00:06:12.937 --> 00:06:16.606
Gently heat the filtrate in an evaporating basin to remove

120
00:06:16.652 --> 00:06:19.717
some of the water and produce a concentrated solution.

121
00:06:20.240 --> 00:06:23.781
Allow the solution to cool so that salt crystals form.

122
00:06:24.175 --> 00:06:28.041
Finally, separate the crystals and dry them using filter paper

123
00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:30.387
or another suitable drying method.

124
00:06:30.909 --> 00:06:34.682
This answer follows a logical sequence, identifies the correct

125
00:06:34.740 --> 00:06:38.746
apparatus and explains the purpose of each important stage.

126
00:06:39.140 --> 00:06:43.390
When answering six-mark questions, always make sure that your explanation

127
00:06:43.506 --> 00:06:47.407
is scientifically accurate, logically organised and sufficiently

128
00:06:47.465 --> 00:06:48.022
detailed.

129
00:06:48.359 --> 00:06:52.631
If you found this lesson useful, please subscribe to J D Science

130
00:06:52.689 --> 00:06:56.323
and visit j d science dot co dot u k for more

131
00:06:56.347 --> 00:07:00.271
G C S E Chemistry explanations, practical investigations

132
00:07:00.422 --> 00:07:02.093
and examination questions.

133
00:07:02.430 --> 00:07:05.147
Thank you for watching, and I look forward to seeing you in

134
00:07:05.182 --> 00:07:06.006
the next lesson.
